/* ===========================================================================
 * Crimson Slate -- the "Ironforge" palette for Noggit Crimson
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Part of Noggit Crimson, licensed under the GNU General Public License v3.
 *
 * The DIRECTORY is still CrimsonSlate and must stay that way: QSettings stores
 * the chosen theme by folder name, so renaming it silently resets every
 * existing user to the default theme. Crimson survives in the palette, but as
 * the DESTRUCTIVE rank only -- the accent is gold.
 *
 * Drop-in theme: the settings panel lists any directory under themes/ that
 * contains a theme.qss. Every url() below is written as
 *   themes/CrimsonSlate/images/<file>.png
 * and must stay that way -- the loader rewrites that exact prefix so the paths
 * resolve against the executable directory rather than the working directory.
 *
 * The 34 PNGs in images/ are generated, not drawn. Edit the colour constants at
 * the top of generate_icons.py (the same tokens as below), then run
 *   python generate_icons.py images
 * from this directory. Stdlib only -- no Pillow, no ImageMagick, no network.
 *
 * check_qss.py in this directory is a static checker for this file. Qt silently
 * ignores anything it does not understand, so run it after every edit:
 *   python check_qss.py theme.qss .
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * PALETTE. Change these values and nothing else to retheme.
 * ===========================================================================
 * The same values build the application QPalette in ApplicationEntry.cpp. If
 * you change one here, change it there too, or an unstyled widget will not
 * match a styled one.
 *
 * HUE DIRECTION. Every neutral is built at hue 30-40 degrees and 6-19%
 * saturation -- warm slate, not photocopier grey. Blizzard chrome is warm iron
 * and old leather lit by gold. Gold is also the only accent that stays fully
 * saturated at the lightness this palette needs; a blue at the same luminance
 * washes out (#6FAEDC is only 61% saturated at L=0.388), so the choice is
 * arithmetic, not taste.
 *
 * SURFACES -- the depth model. The window RECEDES, the panel is the working
 * plane, things you PRESS come forward, fields you TYPE INTO go back to the
 * floor, and things that FLOAT come furthest forward.
 *   bg.void       #100E0B   3D viewport surround, dock gutters, splitters,
 *                           scroll troughs, menu bar, status bar, the title
 *                           bar band -- AND the inside of every input well and
 *                           item view. Also the ink drawn on an accent fill.
 *   bg.alt        #1D1916   alternating item-view row; focused input fill
 *   bg.panel      #292621   dock bodies, dialog bodies, tab panes, sections
 *   bg.raised     #3C3732   buttons, header sections, spin/combo sub-buttons,
 *                           docked toolbars
 *   bg.overlay    #4A4640   menus, combo popups, tool tips, the floating cards
 *                           drawn over the 3D view
 *   bg.hover      #5B5651   hover plate on an overlay surface. ICONS AND SHORT
 *                           LABELS ONLY -- body text on it is 5.47:1 and this
 *                           sheet's floor for prose is 7:1.
 *
 * ADJACENT STEP           MEASURED     PREVIOUS SHEET
 *   void    -> panel      1.279:1      1.064
 *   panel   -> raised     1.281:1      1.101
 *   raised  -> overlay    1.256:1      1.128
 *   overlay -> hover      1.292:1      n/a
 *   void    -> alt        1.104:1      deliberately subtle; zebra only
 *   TOTAL void -> overlay 2.057:1      1.321
 * The old sheet's four "levels" spanned 1.321:1 in total, which is one surface
 * wearing four names -- that is why the application read as a wireframe of
 * itself. This ladder is 2.5x longer in ratio terms.
 *
 * WHY THE TOP SURFACE IS NOT LIGHTER: the 7:1 body-text floor binds.
 * Body text #E4DFD7 on bg.overlay #4A4640 is 7.065:1; on #4B4741 it is 6.955 and
 * fails. Every surface above bg.overlay breaks menu legibility, which is why
 * bg.hover carries icons and short labels and never prose.
 *
 * STROKES
 *   stroke.soft   #403B35   hairline seams INSIDE a panel or a void well.
 *                           1.36:1 on panel, 1.74:1 on void. NOT permitted on
 *                           bg.raised (1.06:1) or bg.overlay (1.18:1).
 *   stroke        #565049   the visible 1px border of a control. 1.89:1 on
 *                           panel, 1.48:1 on raised, 2.42:1 on void.
 *   stroke.hi     #746D64   hover outline, and every separator drawn ON
 *                           bg.overlay. 1.84:1 there, where stroke is 1.18:1.
 *
 * TEXT, four ranks. Measured void / panel / raised / overlay:
 *   text.hi       #F3F0E9   16.93  13.24  10.34   8.23   headings, dock
 *                           titles, values being edited, hovered labels
 *   text          #E4DFD7   14.53  11.36   8.87   7.07   body -- the default
 *   text.dim      #BFB7AA    9.70   7.58   5.92   4.72   captions, units,
 *                           header sections, the unchecked icon pen
 *   text.off      #7F786A    4.40   3.44   2.69   2.14   disabled only
 * Body clears 7:1 on every surface it is permitted on. text.dim clears 4.5:1
 * on all four and is barred from bg.hover (3.65:1). text.off is the one
 * documented exception: WCAG 2.1 SC 1.4.3 exempts inactive components and
 * sub-threshold IS the disabled signal. It is never used for enabled text.
 *
 * ACCENT -- ONE meaning: "the thing you are acting on". Selection, focus,
 * checked, the active tool, slider fill, progress, the tab underline, the dock
 * title rule. The accent moved from crimson to gold because crimson cannot be
 * both "selected" and "will destroy your data": the old accent #E8543F and the
 * old error #E0574B measured 1.024:1 apart in luminance and 4.0 degrees in
 * hue, i.e. the same colour, which is why red meant eight different things.
 * Gold against crimson is 34.5 degrees and 1.389:1.
 *   accent        #DFA52E   ink on it 8.77:1 (crimson managed only 4.88:1)
 *   accent.hi     #F0BA4A   hover on a FILLED accent surface; ink 10.85:1
 *   accent.press  #B8801F   pressed on a FILLED accent surface; ink 5.64:1
 *   ink           #100E0B   = bg.void. The text drawn ON any accent or status
 *                           fill. Note the press no longer has to invert to
 *                           light text the way crimson did.
 * Accent as a 2px/3px RULE on bg.overlay is 4.27:1, over the 3:1 floor for a
 * non-text state indicator. Washes: 0.24 alpha for "selected", 0.34 for
 * "selected AND hovered", permitted on bg.panel and darker only.
 *
 * STATUS -- meaning, never decoration.
 *   ok            #4FB87E   panel 6.09  raised 4.76  ink-on-it 7.79
 *   warn          #E2803C   panel 5.30  raised 4.14  ink-on-it 6.78
 *   bad           #E86F62   panel 4.94  raised 3.86  ink-on-it 6.31
 *   info          #6FAEDC   panel 6.29  raised 4.91  ink-on-it 8.04
 *   bad.press     #C85F54   the destructive button pressed; ink on it 4.79.
 *                           It was #C4574A, which put ink at 4.41 -- under the
 *                           4.5 floor on an ENABLED control, so the disabled
 *                           exemption did not apply. Lightened until ink
 *                           clears, and it is still 1.32:1 below bad, so the
 *                           press step is as legible as it was.
 *   close.hover   #A75047   title-bar close, hovered. See below: that one
 *                           glyph is a polish-time PNG and cannot restate
 *                           itself per pseudo-state, so it stays text.hi and
 *                           the FILL is what moved. text.hi on it 4.75:1, and
 *                           the 40px plate reads 3.56:1 against the bg.void
 *                           bar it sits in.
 *   close.press   #974840   title-bar close, pressed. text.hi 5.56:1, plate
 *                           vs bar 3.04:1, and 1.17:1 below close.hover.
 *   bad.tint      #2E1C17   bad at 14% alpha over bg.void -- an error field
 *   warn.tint     #2D1E12   warn at 14% alpha over bg.void -- a warning field
 * Status colours are TEXT only on bg.void and bg.panel, where all four clear
 * 4.5:1. On bg.raised and bg.overlay they appear as 1px borders and as fills,
 * where the floor is 3:1 and all four clear it.
 * SEPARATION: accent vs bad 34.5 deg / 1.389:1, vs ok 106.5 deg, vs info
 * 165.0 deg. HONEST LIMIT: accent vs warn is 15.8 deg and warn vs bad is 18.8.
 * Four warm signals cannot all be 30 degrees apart. warn and bad are an
 * escalation pair and adjacency is the traffic-light convention. accent vs
 * warn is disambiguated by FORM and this is a hard rule: a warn or error input
 * takes a coloured border AND a 14%-alpha tinted fill; a focused input takes a
 * 1px accent border and NEVER a tint. Never rely on the hue alone.
 *
 * The texture-level slider value ramp, the one gradient in this file:
 *   ramp.hi       #F2F0EC   top of the ramp
 *   ramp.lo       #0C0A08   bottom of the ramp
 * Neither is pure white or pure black -- the editor has to stay legible beside
 * a sunlit viewport, and pure endpoints bloom against it.
 *
 * WORKED ARITHMETIC, so the method can be checked. Relative luminance: per
 * channel c/255, then c<=0.04045 ? c/12.92 : ((c+0.055)/1.055)^2.4;
 * L = 0.2126R + 0.7152G + 0.0722B. Ratio = (Lmax+0.05)/(Lmin+0.05).
 *   1) text #E4DFD7 -> L = 0.741756 ;  bg.panel #292621 -> L = 0.019674
 *      (0.741756+0.05)/(0.019674+0.05)                        = 11.3636:1
 *   2) bg.overlay #4A4640 -> L = 0.062063
 *      (0.741756+0.05)/(0.062063+0.05)                        =  7.0653:1
 *   3) ink #100E0B -> L = 0.004484 ;  accent #DFA52E -> L = 0.427955
 *      (0.427955+0.05)/(0.004484+0.05)                        =  8.7724:1
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * METRICS. One height, three radii, no gradients but the one that is data.
 * ===========================================================================
 *   control height   28px for QPushButton, QLineEdit, QComboBox and
 *                    QAbstractSpinBox. Three classes need three different
 *                    vertical paddings to land there. MEASURED on Qt 5.15.2,
 *                    Fusion, Segoe UI 12px, 1px border, with a standalone
 *                    probe that reports sizeHint and the laid-out height:
 *                      QPushButton   min-height 16 + padding 5px 12px   -> 28
 *                      QLineEdit     padding 1px 8px, min-height 16     -> 28
 *                      QAbstractSpinBox as QLineEdit, padding-right 20  -> 28
 *                      QComboBox     h = 19 + vpad_top + vpad_bottom, so
 *                                    padding 5px 24px 4px 10px          -> 28
 *                    The combo formula in the previous revision of this sheet
 *                    said h = 23 + vpad and would have produced 24px, one
 *                    control four pixels shorter than the button beside it.
 *                    Re-measure before changing any padding here.
 *   radii            CONTROL 5px (buttons, inputs, combos, spin boxes, tool
 *                    buttons, a tab hover plate)
 *                    CONTAINER 8px (item-view wells, floating cards, the
 *                    project list, the minimap holder, text edits)
 *                    POPUP 0px (QMenu, combo popup, QToolTip) -- a rounded
 *                    top-level leaves the corner pixels showing the desktop on
 *                    Windows. Unchanged and correct.
 *                    INDICATOR 4px check box, 8px radio and slider handle
 *                    (half of 16 = a circle), 4px scrollbar thumb.
 *   border widths    1px on every control and hairline. 2px for a tab
 *                    underline and a list row leading rule. 3px for the rule
 *                    that marks the active tool and the dock title. Every
 *                    state RESERVES its rule as transparent, so becoming
 *                    :checked never reflows anything.
 *   QSS min-/max-    size the CONTENT rect, not the box. Measured: a
 *                    QToolButton with min-height 34px, padding 4px and a 1px
 *                    border lays out at 44px, not 34. The 34x34 tool buttons
 *                    below therefore declare the content size that ARRIVES at
 *                    34 after padding and borders, and the arithmetic is shown
 *                    at each rule.
 *
 * SPACING. One 4px scale; every margin, padding and gap comes from it.
 *   s0  2px   a state rule reserve; icon-to-label inside a compact control
 *   s1  4px   inside a UNIT: label to its control, chip to chip, grid gutters
 *   s2  6px   vertical padding in a dense row (menu item, list row, tab)
 *   s3  8px   DEFAULT gap between sibling controls; horizontal input padding;
 *             check box indicator to its text
 *   s4 12px   panel side margins; horizontal button padding; control to rule
 *   s5 16px   between SECTIONS in a panel; dialog content margins
 *   s6 24px   between major regions; dialog outer margins
 *   s7 32px   the column gutter on the project-selection window
 * Use the smallest step that still separates two things that are not part of
 * the same unit. Never an odd number; never a step that is not on this list.
 *
 * TYPE SCALE. Family is Segoe UI; nothing is bundled (see ATTRIBUTION.md).
 * Qt QSS has NO text-transform and NO letter-spacing, so every rank is
 * separable by SIZE and WEIGHT alone and colour only reinforces. No rank
 * shares both a size and a weight with any other.
 *   15/600 text.hi   window titles, the frameless title bar label
 *   14/700 text.hi   QDockWidget::title -- the app answer to "which tool am I
 *                    in", so it out-ranks every heading inside the panel
 *   13/700 text.hi   section headings: QGroupBox::title, QToolBox::tab,
 *                    ads--CDockWidgetTab
 *   12/400 text      the global default: labels, check boxes, buttons, tabs,
 *                    menu items
 *   12/600 text.hi   values: QLabel[state="value"], the selected tab, the
 *                    contents of an input, the viewport overlay label
 *   11/400 text.dim  secondary: captions, units, ranges, the status bar own
 *                    transient showMessage text
 *   11/700 text.dim  QHeaderView::section
 *   12 mono          QTextEdit / QPlainTextEdit -- reports, the Lua log, the
 *                    SQL changeset preview. Consolas; tabular figures matter.
 * GLOBAL SIZE STAYS 12px, which is what Qt 9pt Windows default resolves to at
 * 96 dpi, so the base does not resize and INTERFACE_FONT_PIXEL_SIZE in
 * ApplicationEntry.cpp is unchanged.
 * MEASURED LINE BOXES (QFontMetrics, Segoe UI, standalone probe): 11px -> 13,
 * 12px -> 15, 13px -> 17, 14px -> 19, 15px -> 20, 17px -> 23. Weight does not
 * change any of them. The two rules that had to move for the new sizes are
 * called out at QGroupBox and QDockWidget::title; nothing else reflows.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * The Font Awesome icon font is NOT shipped with this fork. Nothing in this
 * sheet fixes a control size to a glyph, no state is signalled by an icon
 * alone, and the frameless title bar buttons get real PNGs via qproperty-icon
 * below -- so the window controls stay identifiable with no icon font at all.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * NOT REACHABLE FROM QSS -- for whoever wires the 3D side up in C++. Recorded
 * here so the viewport and the chrome stay one design. The accent moved, so
 * these move with it or the viewport still looks crimson.
 *   viewport.active     #DFA52E   accent -- the thing you are acting on
 *   viewport.select     #F0BA4A   accent.hi
 *   spawn.creature      #4FB87E   spawn.gameobject   #A98BE8
 *   waypoint.node/line  #DFA52E   (line at 60% alpha)
 *   chunk.ghost         #DFA52E   db.dirty  #E2803C   db.deleted #E86F62 @40%
 *   layer budget 1-2    #4FB87E @25%   3  #E2803C @30%   4  #E86F62 @35%
 * ======================================================================== */


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- global -- */

QWidget {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	border: none;
	font-family: "Segoe UI";
	font-size: 12px;
	outline: 0;
	selection-background-color: rgba(223, 165, 46, 0.34);
	selection-color: #F3F0E9;
}

QWidget:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}

QDialog {
	background-color: #292621;
}

QMessageBox {
	background-color: #292621;
}

QMessageBox QLabel {
	color: #E4DFD7;
	font-size: 12px;
}

QLabel {
	background: transparent;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	padding: 1px 0px;
}

QLabel:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}

/* A hyperlink is information, not an action, so it takes info and not the
   accent -- the accent means "the thing you are acting on" and nothing else.
   info on bg.panel is 6.29:1. */
QLabel[openExternalLinks="true"] {
	color: #6FAEDC;
}

/* QFrame is a base class, so a bare QFrame rule would repaint every scroll
   area, tree, list and text edit in the app. Only the two line shapes are
   targeted here. */
QFrame[frameShape="4"] {
	background-color: #403B35;
	border: none;
	max-height: 1px;
}

QFrame[frameShape="5"] {
	background-color: #403B35;
	border: none;
	max-width: 1px;
}


/* ------------------------------------------------- window, docks, gutters -- */

/* The window recedes. bg.void is the floor everything else sits on and it is
   what surrounds the 3D viewport: 2.057:1 below the floating cards drawn over
   the render, so a card never washes out against snowy Dun Morogh. */
QMainWindow {
	background-color: #100E0B;
}

QMainWindow::separator {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	width: 4px;
	height: 4px;
}

QMainWindow::separator:hover {
	background-color: #3C3732;
}

QSplitter {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}

QSplitter::handle {
	background-color: #100E0B;
}

QSplitter::handle:horizontal {
	width: 4px;
}

QSplitter::handle:vertical {
	height: 4px;
}

QSplitter::handle:hover {
	background-color: #3C3732;
}

QSplitter::handle:pressed {
	background-color: #DFA52E;
}

QDockWidget {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	font-size: 14px;
	font-weight: 700;
	border: none;
	titlebar-close-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_close.png);
	titlebar-normal-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_restore.png);
}

/* MapView retitles this dock from the active tool on every mode change, so
   this line is the application answer to "which tool am I in" and it has to
   out-rank every heading inside the panel: 14/700 text.hi at 13.24:1 against
   the panel, against 13/700 for a section heading and 12/400 for a label.

   The rule is on the LEFT because a dock title is a horizontal band whose left
   edge is where the eye starts, and a bottom rule would collide with the
   hairline separating the title from the panel.

   REFLOW: the size went 13px -> 14px, whose line box measures 19px against 17.
   Padding drops from 8px/7px to 7px/6px in the same edit, so the bar stays
   19+7+6 = 32 plus the hairline, the height it has always been, and no dock
   moves. */
QDockWidget::title {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: none;
	border-left: 3px solid #DFA52E;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #565049;
	padding: 7px 8px 6px 10px;
	font-size: 14px;
	font-weight: 700;
	text-align: left;
}

QDockWidget::close-button,
QDockWidget::float-button {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 5px;
	width: 22px;
	height: 22px;
	padding: 0px;
	icon-size: 12px;
}

QDockWidget::close-button:hover,
QDockWidget::float-button:hover {
	background-color: #3C3732;
}

QDockWidget::close-button:pressed,
QDockWidget::float-button:pressed {
	background-color: #100E0B;
}


/* ------------------------------------------------------- menus, menu bar -- */

/* The menu bar is part of the window frame, so it sits on bg.void. A selected
   item therefore LIFTS to bg.raised (1.281:1) -- the bar is already the floor
   and there is nothing below it to recess into. */
QMenuBar {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: none;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #403B35;
	padding: 3px 8px;
	spacing: 2px;
}

QMenuBar::item {
	background: transparent;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	padding: 6px 10px;
	margin: 0px 0px;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 5px;
}

QMenuBar::item:selected {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

/* The menu is open: accent at 0.24 over bg.void composites to #423213, a
   1.558:1 plate, with text.hi on it at 10.87:1. */
QMenuBar::item:pressed {
	background-color: rgba(223, 165, 46, 0.24);
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QMenuBar::item:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}

/* Square corners on purpose: a rounded top-level popup leaves the corner
   pixels showing whatever is behind the window on Windows. */
QMenu {
	background-color: #4A4640;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-radius: 0px;
	padding: 5px 4px;
}

QMenu::item {
	background: transparent;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	padding: 6px 30px 6px 30px;
	margin: 1px 3px;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 5px;
}

/* A menu item has no :hover -- Qt marks the item under the cursor :selected --
   so this one rule carries both meanings.

   SELECTION HERE RECESSES rather than tinting, and that is measured rather
   than stylistic: no accent alpha over bg.overlay keeps body text at 7:1
   (0.16 alpha gives 5.49:1, 0.24 gives 4.81:1). Dropping to bg.raised gives
   body text 8.87:1, and the 2px accent left rule reads at 5.36:1 against it.
   This is the one surface in the sheet where selection goes DOWN. */
QMenu::item:selected {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border-left: 2px solid #DFA52E;
	padding-left: 28px;
}

QMenu::item:disabled {
	background: transparent;
	color: #7F786A;
}

/* stroke.hi, not stroke: on bg.overlay stroke measures 1.18:1 and is simply
   not there. stroke.hi is 1.84:1. */
QMenu::separator {
	height: 1px;
	background-color: #746D64;
	margin: 5px 12px;
}

QMenu::indicator {
	width: 14px;
	height: 14px;
	left: 9px;
}

QMenu::indicator:non-exclusive:checked,
QMenu::indicator:exclusive:checked {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_menu_check.png);
}

QMenu::indicator:non-exclusive:checked:disabled,
QMenu::indicator:exclusive:checked:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_menu_check_disabled.png);
}

QMenu::right-arrow {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/right_arrow.png);
	width: 5px;
	height: 8px;
	margin-right: 10px;
}

QMenu::right-arrow:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/right_arrow_disabled.png);
}

QMenu::scroller,
QMenu::tearoff {
	background-color: #4A4640;
}


/* --------------------------------------------------------------- toolbar -- */

/* The DOCKED toolbar. bg.raised, one hairline lid. The floating cards drawn
   over the 3D view are a different thing entirely and are styled at the end of
   this file. */
QToolBar {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	border: none;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #403B35;
	spacing: 4px;
	padding: 4px;
	min-height: 36px;
}

QToolBar:vertical {
	border-bottom: none;
	border-right: 1px solid #403B35;
	min-height: 0px;
}

QToolBar::separator {
	background-color: #565049;
	width: 1px;
	height: 1px;
	margin: 5px 4px;
}

QToolBar::handle:horizontal {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/handle_vertical.png);
	width: 8px;
	margin: 2px 1px;
}

QToolBar::handle:vertical {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/handle_horizontal.png);
	height: 8px;
	margin: 1px 2px;
}


/* ----------------------------------------------------------- status bar -- */

/* Seven readouts, every one a bare QLabel with no object name -- MapView
   builds _status_position, _status_selection, _status_area, _status_time,
   _status_fps, _status_culling and _status_database and hands each straight to
   addWidget. This sheet cannot make one out-rank another; what it can do is
   stop them running together, with a hairline before each field. QSS has no
   :first-child, so the leftmost field also gets a leading rule; it lands
   against the window edge and reads as part of the frame.

   The readouts are text on bg.void (14.53:1); the transient showMessage text
   QStatusBar paints itself stays at 11/400 text.dim (9.70:1), so a five-second
   "Map saved" no longer looks louder than the position.

   Per-field emphasis needs one line of C++ each: QLabel[state="value"] below
   is the mechanism and it carries an attribute selector, so it outranks the
   plain descendant rule here. Set it once at construction on FPS, position and
   the loaded/rendered counts and they lift to 12/600 text.hi. */
QStatusBar {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: none;
	border-top: 1px solid #403B35;
	font-size: 11px;
	min-height: 26px;
	padding: 0px 2px;
}

QStatusBar::item {
	border: none;
}

QStatusBar QLabel {
	color: #E4DFD7;
	border-left: 1px solid #403B35;
	font-size: 11px;
	padding: 2px 10px;
}

QStatusBar QLabel:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}

QSizeGrip {
	background: transparent;
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/sizegrip.png);
	width: 14px;
	height: 14px;
}


/* --------------------------------------------------------------- buttons -- */

/* A button COMES FORWARD (bg.raised) and an input GOES BACK (bg.void). That
   one difference is what stops "press this" and "type here" looking like the
   same object, and it is the reason the previous sheet -- where both sat on
   bg.raised with the same border -- read as a wireframe.

   HOVER LIFTS, PRESS RECESSES. The press drops two whole surface levels to
   bg.void, which is unmistakable against a hover that went up one.

   Geometry is identical in all five states: 1px border everywhere, so nothing
   reflows. Measured height 28px (probe: sizeHint 65x28). */
QPushButton {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	border: 1px solid #565049;
	border-radius: 5px;
	padding: 5px 12px;
	min-height: 16px;
}

QPushButton:hover {
	background-color: #4A4640;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QPushButton:pressed {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #565049;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QPushButton:checked {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QPushButton:checked:hover {
	background-color: #1D1916;
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

/* Focus is a border colour change and nothing else -- no glow, no outline, no
   size change, so nothing reflows when focus moves. */
QPushButton:focus {
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
}

QPushButton:default {
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QPushButton:disabled {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #7F786A;
	border: 1px solid #403B35;
}

QPushButton:flat {
	background: transparent;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
}

QPushButton:flat:hover {
	background-color: #4A4640;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
}

QPushButton:flat:pressed {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QPushButton::menu-indicator {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow.png);
	width: 8px;
	height: 5px;
	subcontrol-origin: padding;
	subcontrol-position: center right;
	right: 6px;
}

QDialogButtonBox QPushButton {
	min-width: 88px;
	padding: 5px 16px;
}

/* Destructive actions. Set from C++ with  btn->setProperty("state", "danger");
   and, if the button already existed, style()->unpolish(btn); polish(btn);

   THE RED IS THE EDGE, NOT THE TEXT, and that is measured: bad #E86F62 as text
   on bg.raised is 3.86:1 and fails the body floor. As a 1px border it clears
   the 3:1 non-text floor, and the label stays text at 8.87:1. Hover is the
   state that commits to the fill, where ink on bad is 6.31:1.

   The press used to darken to #C4574A, which put ink at 4.41:1 -- below the
   4.5 floor, on a control that is ENABLED, so nothing exempted it. The fill
   is now bad.press #C85F54: ink 4.79:1, still 1.32:1 darker than the hover
   fill it steps down from, and 3.75:1 against the bg.panel dialog body. */
QPushButton[state="danger"] {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	border: 1px solid #E86F62;
}

QPushButton[state="danger"]:hover {
	background-color: #E86F62;
	color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #E86F62;
}

QPushButton[state="danger"]:pressed {
	background-color: #C85F54;
	color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #C85F54;
}

/* The confirming action in a two-button row. Set from C++ the same way danger
   is:  btn->setProperty("state", "primary");  and unpolish/polish if the
   button already existed. At most one per window, marking the one action the
   window exists to perform.

   Ink on the fill, not white: #100E0B on #DFA52E is 8.77:1. Gold also removes
   the special case crimson needed -- ink on accent.press is 5.64:1, so the
   press no longer has to invert to light text.

   :disabled is spelled out with two conditions because a bare
   QPushButton:disabled ties with a one-attribute rule on Qt specificity (257
   each) and would then be decided by document order alone. */
QPushButton[state="primary"] {
	background-color: #DFA52E;
	color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	font-weight: 600;
}

QPushButton[state="primary"]:hover {
	background-color: #F0BA4A;
	color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #F0BA4A;
}

QPushButton[state="primary"]:pressed {
	background-color: #B8801F;
	color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #B8801F;
}

QPushButton[state="primary"]:focus {
	border: 1px solid #F3F0E9;
}

QPushButton[state="primary"]:disabled {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #7F786A;
	border: 1px solid #403B35;
}


/* ----------------------------------------------------------- tool buttons -- */

/* The base rule for every QToolButton in the application. HOVER LIFTS, ACTIVE
   RECESSES: the two can never be confused because they move in opposite
   directions. On the default bg.raised surface a hover goes up to bg.overlay
   and a checked button drops to bg.void, a 1.638:1 recess, with a 3px accent
   leading rule at 5.36:1 and a gold glyph.

   Both the 1px border and the 3px border-left are declared in every state at
   those exact widths, so switching tools cannot reflow a bar. Only colours
   move.

   NOTE ON SPECIFICITY, carried forward and still true: an ancestor
   pseudo-state (QToolBar:horizontal QToolButton) is parsed and silently
   DISCARDED by Qt -- QCss::StyleSelector exposes virtuals for a node name, ids
   and attributes but none for its pseudo-state. Scope by object name, class
   name or attribute only. check_qss.py fails the sheet if one reappears. */
QToolButton {
	background: transparent;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 3px solid transparent;
	border-radius: 5px;
	padding: 4px 6px;
	min-width: 24px;
	min-height: 24px;
}

QToolButton:hover {
	background-color: #4A4640;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-left: 3px solid transparent;
}

QToolButton:pressed {
	background-color: #B8801F;
	color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #B8801F;
	border-left: 3px solid #B8801F;
}

QToolButton:checked {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 3px solid #DFA52E;
}

QToolButton:checked:hover {
	background-color: #1D1916;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-left: 3px solid #DFA52E;
}

QToolButton:disabled {
	background: transparent;
	color: #7F786A;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 3px solid transparent;
}

QToolButton[popupMode="1"] {
	padding-right: 18px;
}

QToolButton::menu-button {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 1px solid #565049;
	width: 14px;
}

QToolButton::menu-button:hover {
	background-color: #4A4640;
}

QToolButton::menu-arrow,
QToolButton::menu-indicator {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow.png);
	width: 8px;
	height: 5px;
}

QToolButton::menu-arrow:disabled,
QToolButton::menu-indicator:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow_disabled.png);
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- inputs -- */

/* A FIELD IS A WELL. It goes back to bg.void while a button comes forward to
   bg.raised; that is how the two stop looking alike. 28px tall, 5px radius,
   1px stroke at 2.42:1 against the fill, contents in text.hi at 16.93:1.

   Focus is TWO cues and no reflow: the fill lifts one hair to bg.alt and the
   border turns accent (8.77:1 against the fill). A focused field is NEVER
   tinted -- the tint is reserved for warn and error, and it is what separates
   an orange-bordered field from a gold-bordered one when the two hues are only
   15.8 degrees apart.

   MEASURED heights (Qt 5.15.2 probe, Fusion, Segoe UI 12px, 1px border):
     QLineEdit        padding 1px 8px, min-height 16   -> 28
     QAbstractSpinBox as above plus padding-right 20   -> 28
     QComboBox        h = 19 + vpad_top + vpad_bottom  -> 5px/4px lands on 28
   Change a padding here and you have changed the height; re-measure. */
QLineEdit,
QTextEdit,
QPlainTextEdit,
QAbstractSpinBox,
QComboBox {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #565049;
	border-radius: 5px;
	font-weight: 600;
	selection-background-color: rgba(223, 165, 46, 0.34);
	selection-color: #F3F0E9;
}

QLineEdit,
QAbstractSpinBox {
	padding: 1px 8px;
	min-height: 16px;
}

QLineEdit:hover,
QTextEdit:hover,
QPlainTextEdit:hover,
QAbstractSpinBox:hover,
QComboBox:hover {
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
}

QLineEdit:focus,
QTextEdit:focus,
QPlainTextEdit:focus,
QAbstractSpinBox:focus,
QComboBox:focus {
	background-color: #1D1916;
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
}

QLineEdit:disabled,
QTextEdit:disabled,
QPlainTextEdit:disabled,
QAbstractSpinBox:disabled,
QComboBox:disabled {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #7F786A;
	border: 1px solid #403B35;
}

QLineEdit:read-only {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: 1px solid #403B35;
	font-weight: 400;
}

/* Reports, the Lua console log and any SQL preview want tabular figures. They
   are containers rather than single-line controls, so they take the 8px
   container radius and body weight. */
QTextEdit,
QPlainTextEdit {
	font-family: "Consolas";
	font-size: 12px;
	font-weight: 400;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	border-radius: 8px;
	padding: 8px;
}

/* Error and warning states on an input. Set from C++ with
   setProperty("state", "error") / ("state", "warn") plus unpolish/polish.

   THE TINT IS LOAD-BEARING. Border alone is not enough: warn #E2803C and the
   accent #DFA52E are 15.8 degrees apart, so a bordered-only warn field and a
   focused field would be told apart by hue alone. The 14%-alpha fill over
   bg.void is a difference in KIND. Composited: bad -> #2E1C17, warn ->
   #2D1E12; the border reads 5.32:1 and 5.66:1 against its own fill and the
   contents stay at 14.25:1 and 14.13:1. */
QLineEdit[state="error"],
QAbstractSpinBox[state="error"],
QComboBox[state="error"] {
	background-color: #2E1C17;
	border: 1px solid #E86F62;
}

QLineEdit[state="warn"],
QAbstractSpinBox[state="warn"],
QComboBox[state="warn"] {
	background-color: #2D1E12;
	border: 1px solid #E2803C;
}

QLabel[state="error"] {
	color: #E86F62;
}

QLabel[state="warn"] {
	color: #E2803C;
}

QLabel[state="ok"] {
	color: #4FB87E;
}

/* A label carrying a live value -- the status bar readouts, the current
   radius, a spawn GUID. The "value" rank: 12/600 text.hi. */
QLabel[state="value"] {
	color: #F3F0E9;
	font-size: 12px;
	font-weight: 600;
}


/* ------------------------------------------------------------ spin boxes -- */

/* The steppers are a strip inside the well rather than two chiclets bolted to
   it: no fill of their own until hovered, one 1px divider. */
QAbstractSpinBox {
	padding-right: 20px;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::up-button {
	subcontrol-origin: border;
	subcontrol-position: top right;
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 1px solid #565049;
	border-top-right-radius: 4px;
	width: 17px;
	margin: 1px 1px 0px 0px;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::down-button {
	subcontrol-origin: border;
	subcontrol-position: bottom right;
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 1px solid #565049;
	border-bottom-right-radius: 4px;
	width: 17px;
	margin: 0px 1px 1px 0px;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::up-button:hover,
QAbstractSpinBox::down-button:hover {
	background-color: #4A4640;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::up-button:pressed,
QAbstractSpinBox::down-button:pressed {
	background-color: #B8801F;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::up-button:disabled,
QAbstractSpinBox::down-button:disabled {
	background: transparent;
	border-left: 1px solid #403B35;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::up-arrow {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/up_arrow.png);
	width: 8px;
	height: 5px;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::down-arrow {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow.png);
	width: 8px;
	height: 5px;
}

QAbstractSpinBox::up-arrow:disabled,
QAbstractSpinBox::up-arrow:off {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/up_arrow_disabled.png);
}

QAbstractSpinBox::down-arrow:disabled,
QAbstractSpinBox::down-arrow:off {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow_disabled.png);
}


/* -------------------------------------------------------------- combo box -- */

/* 5px top / 4px bottom is measured, not chosen: a combo lays out at
   19 + vpad_top + vpad_bottom, so 9px of vertical padding is the only way to
   land on the same 28px as the button beside it. A sweep of every pair from
   3/2 to 7/6 was run through the probe; 3/2 -- what the design note asked for
   -- produces 24px. */
QComboBox {
	padding: 5px 24px 4px 10px;
	min-height: 16px;
}

QComboBox:on {
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
}

QComboBox::drop-down {
	subcontrol-origin: border;
	subcontrol-position: top right;
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 1px solid #565049;
	border-top-right-radius: 4px;
	border-bottom-right-radius: 4px;
	width: 20px;
	margin: 1px;
}

QComboBox::drop-down:hover {
	background-color: #4A4640;
}

QComboBox::drop-down:disabled {
	background: transparent;
	border-left: 1px solid #403B35;
}

QComboBox::down-arrow {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow.png);
	width: 8px;
	height: 5px;
}

QComboBox::down-arrow:on {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/up_arrow.png);
}

QComboBox::down-arrow:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow_disabled.png);
}

/* The popup is a separate top-level widget and does not inherit the combo own
   rule, so it has to be styled by name. It is a popup: bg.overlay, stroke.hi,
   square corners. Selection RECESSES to bg.raised for the same measured reason
   the menu does -- no accent alpha over bg.overlay keeps body text at 7:1. */
QComboBox QAbstractItemView {
	background-color: #4A4640;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-radius: 0px;
	padding: 4px;
	outline: 0;
	selection-background-color: #3C3732;
	selection-color: #F3F0E9;
}


/* ------------------------------------------------- check boxes and radios -- */

/* The nine-radio brush type grid is the most prominent control in the editor.
   The indicator is a 16px well with a 2px stroke ring, flooded accent with an
   ink glyph when checked. All artwork is generated PNG: change the constants
   in generate_icons.py and re-run it, never hand-draw.

   Label colour follows the four ranks: text at rest, text.hi on hover and when
   checked, text.off when disabled. */
QCheckBox,
QRadioButton {
	background: transparent;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	spacing: 8px;
	padding: 3px 0px;
	border: none;
}

QCheckBox:hover,
QRadioButton:hover {
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QCheckBox:checked,
QRadioButton:checked {
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QCheckBox:disabled,
QRadioButton:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}

QCheckBox::indicator,
QRadioButton::indicator,
QGroupBox::indicator {
	width: 16px;
	height: 16px;
}

QCheckBox::indicator:unchecked,
QGroupBox::indicator:unchecked {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_unchecked.png);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:unchecked:hover,
QGroupBox::indicator:unchecked:hover {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_unchecked_hover.png);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:unchecked:disabled,
QGroupBox::indicator:unchecked:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_unchecked_disabled.png);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:checked,
QGroupBox::indicator:checked {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_checked.png);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:checked:hover,
QGroupBox::indicator:checked:hover {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_checked_hover.png);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:checked:disabled,
QGroupBox::indicator:checked:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_checked_disabled.png);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:indeterminate {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_indeterminate.png);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:indeterminate:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_checkbox_indeterminate_disabled.png);
}

QRadioButton::indicator:unchecked {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_radiobutton_unchecked.png);
}

QRadioButton::indicator:unchecked:hover {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_radiobutton_unchecked_hover.png);
}

QRadioButton::indicator:unchecked:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_radiobutton_unchecked_disabled.png);
}

QRadioButton::indicator:checked {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_radiobutton_checked.png);
}

QRadioButton::indicator:checked:hover {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_radiobutton_checked_hover.png);
}

QRadioButton::indicator:checked:disabled {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_radiobutton_checked_disabled.png);
}


/* -------------------------------------------------------------- group box -- */

/* Stock Qt draws a group box as a bordered rectangle with a centred bold
   underlined title, and the tool panels are nothing but group boxes, so the
   right-hand dock would read as a stack of 1998 dialog frames. Here there is
   no box: the title is a heading in the widget own top margin, one rule runs
   under it, and that rule is the only mark.

   The rule is stroke #565049 and not stroke.soft. On bg.panel stroke.soft is
   1.36:1 and stroke is 1.89:1, and this is the application primary structural
   divider -- the quietest line that can still be found without knowing it is
   there.

   REFLOW, stated so nobody is surprised: the title goes 11/700 -> 13/700, the
   section-heading rank. Its line box measures 17px (probe: QFontMetrics, Segoe
   UI 13px, any weight). margin-top therefore goes 20px -> 22px and the title
   padding-bottom 6px -> 5px, so 17 + 5 = 22 fits the margin exactly. Every
   group section grows by 2px and nothing else moves. */
QGroupBox {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-top: 1px solid #565049;
	border-radius: 0px;
	margin-top: 22px;
	margin-bottom: 4px;
	padding: 12px 0px 12px 0px;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	font-size: 13px;
	font-weight: 700;
	spacing: 8px;
}

QGroupBox::title {
	subcontrol-origin: margin;
	subcontrol-position: top left;
	background: transparent;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	left: 0px;
	padding: 0px 8px 5px 0px;
	font-size: 13px;
	font-weight: 700;
}

QGroupBox::title:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}

/* A disabled section drops one step down the stroke ramp. */
QGroupBox:disabled {
	border-top: 1px solid #403B35;
}

/* A group box nested inside another one gets no rule of its own, or the panel
   turns into a ladder. */
QGroupBox QGroupBox {
	border-top: none;
	margin-top: 16px;
	padding-top: 4px;
}

/* A checkable group box needs its indicator clear of the title text. */
QGroupBox::indicator {
	margin-right: 6px;
}


/* ------------------------------------------------------------------- tabs -- */

/* No raised boxes and no pane frame: the selected tab is marked by a 2px
   accent rule on its own edge (6.86:1 against the panel) and by the value
   rank, 12/600 text.hi. The 2px rule is reserved as transparent at rest, so
   selecting a tab never moves the strip. */
QTabWidget::pane {
	background-color: #292621;
	border: none;
	border-top: 1px solid #403B35;
	border-radius: 0px;
	top: -1px;
}

QTabWidget::tab-bar {
	left: 0px;
}

QTabBar {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	qproperty-drawBase: 0;
}

QTabBar::tab {
	background: transparent;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: none;
	border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
	padding: 8px 16px;
	min-width: 52px;
	font-size: 12px;
	font-weight: 400;
}

QTabBar::tab:hover {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	border-top-left-radius: 5px;
	border-top-right-radius: 5px;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QTabBar::tab:selected {
	background: transparent;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #DFA52E;
	font-weight: 600;
}

QTabBar::tab:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}

QTabBar::tab:left,
QTabBar::tab:right {
	border-bottom: none;
	padding: 16px 8px;
	min-width: 0px;
	min-height: 52px;
}

QTabBar::tab:left {
	border-right: 2px solid transparent;
}

QTabBar::tab:left:selected {
	border-right: 2px solid #DFA52E;
}

QTabBar::tab:right {
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
}

QTabBar::tab:right:selected {
	border-left: 2px solid #DFA52E;
}

QTabBar::close-button {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_close.png);
	subcontrol-position: right;
	margin: 2px;
}

QTabBar::close-button:hover {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_close_hover.png);
}

QTabBar QToolButton {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	border: 1px solid #565049;
	border-left: 1px solid #565049;
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- toolbox -- */

/* A toolbox tab is a section heading, so it takes the section-heading rank
   verbatim: 13/700, a 2px leading rule reserved transparent, and the same
   recess-plus-accent mark every other selected thing in the sheet uses. */
QToolBox {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}

QToolBox::tab {
	background: transparent;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: none;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #403B35;
	padding: 8px 10px;
	font-size: 13px;
	font-weight: 700;
}

QToolBox::tab:hover {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QToolBox::tab:selected {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border-left: 2px solid #DFA52E;
}

QToolBox::tab:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}


/* ------------------------------------------------------------- item views -- */

/* AN ITEM VIEW IS A WELL, the same shape an input is: bg.void inside a
   stroke.soft hairline at the 8px container radius. That is what fixes the
   project-row contrast matrix for free -- over a bg.void well the worst
   reading in the whole five-state selection matrix is 4.88:1, against 3.80:1
   in the previous sheet, which is why the extra text.mid token this sheet used
   to carry has been deleted rather than maintained.

   Selection is an accent WASH over the well and a 2px accent leading rule; in
   the texture and asset browsers the cell contents are the data, so those keep
   an outline instead and are overridden further down. */
QAbstractItemView {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	alternate-background-color: #1D1916;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	border: 1px solid #403B35;
	border-radius: 8px;
	padding: 4px;
	outline: 0;
	show-decoration-selected: 1;
	selection-background-color: rgba(223, 165, 46, 0.24);
	selection-color: #F3F0E9;
}

QAbstractItemView:disabled {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #7F786A;
	border: 1px solid #403B35;
}

QAbstractItemView::item {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
	padding: 6px 8px;
	min-height: 24px;
}

/* A row hover LIFTS from the well to bg.panel, a 1.279:1 step. */
QAbstractItemView::item:hover {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

/* Four distinct selection states, not one. Without :!active a panel that has
   lost focus still screams "this row is selected" as loudly as the one you are
   actually working in.

   Measured over the bg.void well: 0.24 alpha composites to #423213, a 1.558:1
   plate carrying text.hi at 10.87:1 and text.dim at 6.23:1. Hovered as well,
   0.34 gives #564117, 1.986:1, text.hi 8.53:1 and text.dim 4.88:1. */
QAbstractItemView::item:selected:active {
	background-color: rgba(223, 165, 46, 0.24);
	border-left: 2px solid #DFA52E;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QAbstractItemView::item:selected:!active {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	border-left: 2px solid #746D64;
	color: #E4DFD7;
}

QAbstractItemView::item:selected:hover {
	background-color: rgba(223, 165, 46, 0.34);
	border-left: 2px solid #DFA52E;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QAbstractItemView::item:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}

QListView {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #403B35;
}

QListView::item {
	min-height: 24px;
}

QTreeView {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #403B35;
	paint-alternating-row-colors-for-empty-area: 0;
}

QTreeView::item {
	padding: 5px 4px;
}

/* No dotted connector lines. The chevron alone is the affordance, which is
   what survives at small sizes. */
QTreeView::branch {
	background: transparent;
	border-image: none;
	image: none;
}

QTreeView::branch:has-children:!has-siblings:closed,
QTreeView::branch:closed:has-children:has-siblings {
	border-image: none;
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_branch_closed.png);
}

QTreeView::branch:open:has-children:!has-siblings,
QTreeView::branch:open:has-children:has-siblings {
	border-image: none;
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_branch_open.png);
}

QTreeView::branch:selected {
	background-color: rgba(223, 165, 46, 0.24);
}

QTableView {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	alternate-background-color: #1D1916;
	gridline-color: #403B35;
	border: 1px solid #403B35;
	selection-background-color: rgba(223, 165, 46, 0.24);
	selection-color: #F3F0E9;
}

QTableView QTableCornerButton::section {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	border: none;
	border-right: 1px solid #403B35;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #403B35;
}

QHeaderView {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}

/* The table-header rank: 11/700 text.dim, 5.92:1 on bg.raised. A header is a
   label for a column, not content, so it is the one place in the sheet where a
   700 weight sits at the smallest size. */
QHeaderView::section {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: none;
	border-right: 1px solid #403B35;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #403B35;
	padding: 6px 8px;
	min-height: 26px;
	font-size: 11px;
	font-weight: 700;
}

QHeaderView::section:hover {
	background-color: #4A4640;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QHeaderView::section:checked {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #DFA52E;
}

QHeaderView::section:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}

QHeaderView::section:last,
QHeaderView::section:only-one {
	border-right: none;
}

QHeaderView::down-arrow {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/down_arrow.png);
	width: 8px;
	height: 5px;
	subcontrol-origin: content;
	subcontrol-position: center right;
	right: 4px;
}

QHeaderView::up-arrow {
	image: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/up_arrow.png);
	width: 8px;
	height: 5px;
	subcontrol-origin: content;
	subcontrol-position: center right;
	right: 4px;
}


/* ------------------------------------------------------------ scroll bars -- */

/* 12px, not 10: ten pixels is under every platform minimum comfortable drag
   target. No arrows, no page fill. The trough is bg.void so it disappears into
   the chrome; only the thumb is visible and only the thumb moves. */
QScrollBar:vertical {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: none;
	width: 12px;
	margin: 0px;
}

QScrollBar:horizontal {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: none;
	height: 12px;
	margin: 0px;
}

QScrollBar::handle:vertical {
	background-color: #565049;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 4px;
	margin: 2px;
	min-height: 32px;
}

QScrollBar::handle:horizontal {
	background-color: #565049;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 4px;
	margin: 2px;
	min-width: 32px;
}

QScrollBar::handle:hover {
	background-color: #746D64;
}

QScrollBar::handle:pressed {
	background-color: #DFA52E;
}

QScrollBar::handle:disabled {
	background-color: #292621;
}

QScrollBar::add-line,
QScrollBar::sub-line {
	background: none;
	border: none;
	width: 0px;
	height: 0px;
}

QScrollBar::add-page,
QScrollBar::sub-page {
	background: transparent;
}

QScrollBar::up-arrow,
QScrollBar::down-arrow,
QScrollBar::left-arrow,
QScrollBar::right-arrow {
	background: none;
	image: none;
	width: 0px;
	height: 0px;
}

QAbstractScrollArea::corner {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: none;
}


/* --------------------------------------------------------------- sliders -- */

/* Every Radius / Inner Radius / Speed row in the tool panels ends in one of
   these, so it is the most repeated control in the editor after the label. The
   filled portion is accent at 8.77:1 against the bg.void groove: the groove is
   a readout, not a rail.

   The handle is a 16px round grip -- 12px of fill plus a 2px bg.void ring each
   side, radius 8, which is half of 16 and therefore a full circle. margin -5px
   makes it overhang the 6px groove by 5px on each side so it reads as ON the
   track. The ring gives 16.93:1 against the groove and separates the grip from
   the accent fill it sits at the end of.

   ONLY THE FILL AND THE RING MOVE BETWEEN STATES. The 16px box and the radius
   are declared once on the base rules and never restated, so a hover cannot
   resize the grip and drag the row with it.

   HONEST LIMIT, unchanged: Qt derives SC_SliderHandle from the WIDGET height,
   never from the QSS handle box, so this changes what is PAINTED and not the
   hit target. Nothing about the range, the step, the page step or the emitted
   value moves either way. */
QSlider {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}

QSlider::groove:horizontal {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 3px;
	height: 6px;
	margin: 0px 2px;
}

QSlider::sub-page:horizontal {
	background-color: #DFA52E;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 3px;
}

QSlider::add-page:horizontal {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 3px;
}

QSlider::handle:horizontal {
	background-color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 2px solid #100E0B;
	border-radius: 8px;
	width: 12px;
	height: 12px;
	margin: -5px 0px;
}

QSlider::groove:vertical {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 3px;
	width: 6px;
	margin: 2px 0px;
}

QSlider::sub-page:vertical {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 3px;
}

QSlider::add-page:vertical {
	background-color: #DFA52E;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 3px;
}

QSlider::handle:vertical {
	background-color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 2px solid #100E0B;
	border-radius: 8px;
	height: 12px;
	width: 12px;
	margin: 0px -5px;
}

/* Hover keeps the fill and moves the RING to accent, so the box never resizes
   and the state is still legible at the end of the filled run. */
QSlider::handle:hover {
	background-color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 2px solid #DFA52E;
}

QSlider::handle:pressed {
	background-color: #DFA52E;
	border: 2px solid #100E0B;
}

/* The ring goes with it, or a disabled handle keeps a hard black outline and
   still reads as the liveliest thing in a greyed-out group. */
QSlider::handle:disabled {
	background-color: #565049;
	border: 2px solid #292621;
}

QSlider::sub-page:horizontal:disabled,
QSlider::add-page:vertical:disabled {
	background-color: #565049;
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------- progress bar -- */

QProgressBar {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	color: #E4DFD7;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 5px;
	text-align: center;
	font-size: 11px;
	min-height: 20px;
}

QProgressBar::chunk {
	background-color: #DFA52E;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 5px;
}

QProgressBar:disabled {
	color: #7F786A;
}

QProgressBar::chunk:disabled {
	background-color: #565049;
}


/* -------------------------------------------------------------- tool tip -- */

/* A tool tip floats, so it takes bg.overlay and stroke.hi like every other
   popup. text.hi on it is 8.23:1; a multi-line tip that falls back to body
   text is still 7.07:1. */
QToolTip {
	background-color: #4A4640;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-radius: 0px;
	padding: 6px 10px;
	font-size: 12px;
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------- scroll area seams -- */

/* QScrollArea, its viewport and its content widget are three separate widgets.
   Left alone they pick up three different backgrounds and you get a visible
   seam down the middle of every tool panel. Item views are QAbstractScrollArea
   and not QScrollArea, so they keep the well surface above. */
QScrollArea {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}

QScrollArea > QWidget > QWidget {
	background: transparent;
}

/* The right-hand tool dock. The ADS name is kept so the rule is still there if
   the Advanced Docking System is ever switched on; the scroll area the tool
   dock actually builds is named "scrollArea" in ToolPanelScroll.ui.

   HONEST LIMIT, restated: whether Qt honours `padding` as a layout inset on a
   QScrollArea is not something this sheet can prove and it was not verified
   against a running editor. The reliable fix for the missing panel margin is
   contentsMargins(12, 8, 12, 8) in the FORM, which is a different file and is
   not owned by this pass. Do not read this rule as evidence that the padding
   is applied. */
QScrollArea#dockWidgetScrollArea,
QScrollArea#scrollArea {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	padding: 0px 4px;
}

QWidget#scrollAreaWidgetContents {
	background: transparent;
	padding: 0px 4px;
}


/* ================================================================ Noggit == */
/* Application-specific selectors. Class names use Qt convention of "--" for
   "::", so Noggit::Ui::PaletteList is Noggit--Ui--PaletteList. The shipped
   themes spelled these with a single dash in the first position, which matches
   nothing; both spellings are listed here so neither is silently dead.       */

/* --- the frameless window title bar ------------------------------------- */

/* Only ever on screen when systemWindowFrame is off, which is not the default.
   Styled all the same, because a half-dressed alternative is worse than no
   alternative. setupUi names the holder "TitleBar" and FramelessWindow.hpp
   sets WA_StyledBackground on it, so this rule paints at all. bg.void: a title
   bar is the outermost chrome there is. */
QWidget#TitleBar {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: none;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #403B35;
}

/* The window-title rank: 15/600 text.hi, 16.93:1 on bg.void. It used to
   inherit the plain 12/400 body rule, so the name of the window carried the
   same weight as a check box caption. */
QLabel[accessibleName="titlebar_title"] {
	background: transparent;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	font-size: 15px;
	font-weight: 600;
	padding-left: 2px;
}

QLabel[accessibleName="titlebar_icon"],
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_icon"] {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	margin-left: 8px;
	margin-right: 6px;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_minimize"],
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_maximize"],
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_restore"],
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_close"] {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 0px;
	min-width: 40px;
	padding: 0px;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_minimize"]:hover,
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_maximize"]:hover,
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_restore"]:hover {
	background-color: #3C3732;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_minimize"]:pressed,
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_maximize"]:pressed,
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_restore"]:pressed {
	background-color: #4A4640;
}

/* Close is the one control that goes red on hover, because it is the one
   control you cannot undo.

   WHY THE FILL IS NOT bad. This button's glyph is a PNG named by
   qproperty-icon, and Qt resolves qproperty-* once at polish time -- it does
   not restate itself when a pseudo-state changes. So ONE pen has to serve the
   resting bar and every fill, and the resting bar is bg.void: an ink glyph is
   1.00:1 there, i.e. invisible, so the pen has to be light and is text.hi.
   The fill is therefore the half that had to move. It was bad #E86F62, on
   which text.hi measures 2.68:1 -- and the comment that used to sit here
   claimed "ink on bad, 6.31:1", describing a glyph this theme has never
   shipped. generate_icons.py writes that pixel as text.hi and says so.

   Measured on what is actually drawn:
     text.hi #F3F0E9 on close.hover #A75047   4.75:1   (was 2.68 on bad)
     text.hi #F3F0E9 on close.press #974840   5.56:1   (was 3.84)
   and the plate itself is a graphical state mark against the bg.void bar, so
   it carries the 3:1 floor rather than 4.5:
     close.hover vs bg.void  3.56:1     close.press vs bg.void  3.04:1
   with close.press 1.17:1 below close.hover so the press still reads as a
   step. Both fills keep bad's hue; they are darker, not greyer. */
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_close"]:hover {
	background-color: #A75047;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_close"]:pressed {
	background-color: #974840;
}

/* Real PNG icons rather than Font Awesome glyphs, so the window controls are
   identifiable with no icon font installed. The maximise control has TWO
   names: FramelessWindow.hpp renames it to titlebar_restore whenever the
   window reports itself maximised and repolishes, which is how the icon swaps
   without a setIcon that the next polish would undo. */
QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_minimize"] {
	qproperty-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_window_minimize.png);
	qproperty-iconSize: 12px 12px;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_maximize"] {
	qproperty-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_window_maximize.png);
	qproperty-iconSize: 11px 11px;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_restore"] {
	qproperty-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_restore.png);
	qproperty-iconSize: 11px 11px;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="titlebar_close"] {
	qproperty-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_window_close.png);
	qproperty-iconSize: 12px 12px;
}

/* --- icon-font buttons --------------------------------------------------- */

/* THESE TWO RULES ARE NOT ORDINARY WIDGET STYLING. They are the only input to
   the pen colour of every icon in the application. Both icon engines resolve
   it by polishing one parentless probe widget carrying one of these accessible
   names and reading its QPalette back -- see `iconPenColor` in
   FontAwesome.hpp -- and because QSS `color` writes WindowText, Text and
   ButtonText together, a sheet gets exactly TWO slots here and there is no
   third. The engine spends them as:

     this rule            QIcon::On   = CHECKED
     the :disabled rule   QIcon::Off  = UNCHECKED, and also QIcon::Disabled,
                          which the engine separates by painting the same pen
                          at ICON_DISABLED_OPACITY = 0.45

   `color` on a QToolButton rule elsewhere in this file colours that button
   TEXT and cannot reach the glyph. Hover is not a slot at all: Qt reports a
   hovered button as QIcon::Active and the engine derives it as
   QColor::lighter(125) on whichever pen the state resolved to.

   OFF is text.dim #BFB7AA. WHY NOT text OR text.hi -- two reasons, both
   measured. First, lighter(125) on text #E4DFD7 CLIPS TO PURE #FFFFFF (HSL
   lightness 0.869 x 1.25 = 1.000) and text.hi clips too, and this palette
   forbids pure white; #BFB7AA has HSL lightness 0.708, lifts to 0.885 and
   lands on #E6E3DD with room to spare, so it is the only candidate in the text
   ramp that gives a working hover at all. Second, thirty-six icons are on
   screen at once (16 tools plus 20 toggles); at `text` they would all sit at
   7.07:1 and the two bars would be the loudest thing in the application.

   MEASURED, per surface an icon actually rests on:

                       OFF #BFB7AA   HOVER #E6E3DD   ON #DFA52E
     bg.void  #100E0B      9.70          15.05          8.77
     bg.alt   #1D1916      8.79          13.63          7.95
     bg.panel #292621      7.58          11.77          6.86
     bg.raised#3C3732      5.92           9.19          5.36
     bg.overlay #4A4640    4.72           7.32          4.27
     bg.hover #5B5651      3.65           5.66          3.30

   The OFF -> HOVER step between the two pens is 1.551:1, a real and visible
   response. OFF -> CHECKED is only 1.106:1 in luminance but 105 degrees in
   hue, and it is never asked to carry the state alone: the checked face drops
   2.057:1 and a 2px or 3px accent rule appears. The pen is the supporting cue.
   Checked-and-hovered derives to #E8BE68, 9.97:1 on bg.alt.

   DISABLED is the OFF pen at 0.45 alpha: #5F5A53 on bg.void (2.82:1), #6C675F
   on bg.panel (2.69:1), #777168 on bg.raised (2.44:1), #7F7970 on bg.overlay
   (2.17:1). Deliberately sub-threshold -- that IS the disabled signal and WCAG
   1.4.3 exempts inactive components. What matters is that it is no longer
   identical to unchecked; the 0.45 alpha now has a 2.2x difference to
   express. */
QWidget[accessibleName="FontNoggitButtonStyle"],
QWidget[accessibleName="FontAwesomeButtonStyle"] {
	color: #DFA52E;
}

QWidget[accessibleName="FontNoggitButtonStyle"]:disabled,
QWidget[accessibleName="FontAwesomeButtonStyle"]:disabled {
	color: #BFB7AA;
}

/* --- the viewport overlay ------------------------------------------------ */

/* The overlay itself is translucent, but its layout holders are plain QWidgets
   and would otherwise paint opaque slabs of panel colour over the 3D view. */
QWidget#MapViewOverlay,
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder,
QWidget#upperToolbarHolder,
QWidget#secondaryToolbarHolder,
QWidget#leftSecondaryToolbarHolder {
	background: transparent;
}

/* FLOATING VIEWPORT CARDS. QSS has no box-shadow; the dark fill, the stroke.hi
   edge and the 8px container radius are what separate a card from the render
   behind it. bg.overlay is 2.057:1 lighter than the application own darkest
   chrome and far darker than snow, so against snowy Dun Morogh or sunlit
   Arathi the card never washes out.

   KNOWN DEAD, LEFT IN PLACE: Noggit--Ui--toolbar matches nothing. Qt resolves
   a QSS type selector against metaObject()->className() and Noggit::Ui::toolbar
   carries no Q_OBJECT macro, so its class name is literally "QToolBar". The
   strip is reached by POSITION instead -- QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar
   -- which outranks both QToolBar (1) and QToolBar:vertical (257) at 65538 and
   therefore wins by cascade rather than by document order. */
Noggit--Ui--toolbar,
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar,
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar,
QWidget#gizmoBar {
	background-color: #4A4640;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-radius: 8px;
	padding: 5px;
	spacing: 2px;
	min-height: 0px;
}

/* --- the left tool strip ------------------------------------------------- */

/* Sixteen editing tools down the left edge of the viewport in one
   QActionGroup, so exactly one is checked. HOVER LIFTS, ACTIVE RECESSES.

     INACTIVE      transparent, the card shows through. Icon text.dim, 4.72:1.
     HOVER         bg.hover #5B5651, a 1.292:1 LIFT over the card, 1px
                   stroke.hi. Icon derives to #E6E3DD, 5.66:1.
     ACTIVE        bg.void #100E0B, a 2.057:1 RECESS below the card, 3px accent
                   leading rule at 4.27:1 against the card, icon accent at
                   8.77:1 on the recessed face. Three simultaneous cues, and
                   against a hovered neighbour that has gone UP one level the
                   direction alone is unmistakable.
     ACTIVE+HOVER  bg.alt #1D1916, 1.863:1 -- still clearly down, but lifted
                   from the resting active state so the hover is felt. Icon
                   accent 7.95:1.
     PRESSED       accent.press fill, the 3px accent rule held at full strength
                   so a click cannot erase the state it is about to change.
                   Icon ink 5.64:1.
     DISABLED      transparent everywhere, no state mark, icon at 0.45 alpha.

   BUTTON BOX 34x34, and it has to be written here because the base QToolButton
   rule min-width/min-height of 24px overrides any setFixedSize in C++. QSS
   min-/max- size the CONTENT rect, so the box arithmetic is
     width  = 3 (left border) + 3 (pad) + 22 (content) + 5 (pad) + 1 = 34
     height = 1 + 4 + 24 + 4 + 1                                     = 34
   Measured with the standalone probe: actual 34x34. The asymmetric horizontal
   padding pays for the 3px leading rule, so the 20px icon still centres.

   Every state restates border 1px and border-left 3px at those exact widths,
   so switching tools cannot reflow the strip; only colours move. */
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton {
	background: transparent;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 3px solid transparent;
	border-radius: 5px;
	padding: 4px 5px 4px 3px;
	min-width: 22px;
	max-width: 22px;
	min-height: 24px;
	max-height: 24px;
	icon-size: 20px;
}

QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton:hover {
	background-color: #5B5651;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-left: 3px solid transparent;
}

QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton:checked {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 3px solid #DFA52E;
}

/* Both marks at once. Two pseudo-states, so it outranks the two single-state
   rules above whichever order Qt walks them in. */
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton:checked:hover {
	background-color: #1D1916;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-left: 3px solid #DFA52E;
}

/* :pressed ties with :hover on specificity and wins on document order, and
   :checked:pressed ties with :checked:hover the same way, so all four are
   spelled out rather than left to chance. */
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton:pressed {
	background-color: #B8801F;
	color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #B8801F;
	border-left: 3px solid #B8801F;
}

QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton:checked:pressed {
	background-color: #B8801F;
	color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #B8801F;
	border-left: 3px solid #DFA52E;
}

/* Last of the single-pseudo rules, so a disabled tool shows no state mark. */
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar QToolButton:disabled {
	background: transparent;
	color: #7F786A;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 3px solid transparent;
}

/* The strip group separators sit on bg.overlay, where `stroke` is 1.18:1 and
   simply not there. stroke.hi is 1.84:1. */
QWidget#leftToolbarHolder > QToolBar::separator {
	background-color: #746D64;
	margin: 4px 5px;
}

/* --- the view-toggle bar ------------------------------------------------- */

/* The 20 checkable buttons across the top of the viewport: Doodads, WMOs, WMO
   doodads, WMO exterior, Terrain, Water, Lines, Hole lines, Wireframe,
   Contours, Climb, Vertex Color, Baked Shadows, Animations, Fog, Flight
   bounds, Models with box, Hidden models, Show Minimap, Details info. Twenty
   is a count of the live add_tool_icon calls in the second ViewToolbar
   constructor; the twenty-first button on the bar is the history QPushButton,
   which is not checkable. EIGHT are ON at startup -- _draw_models, _draw_wmo,
   _draw_wmo_doodads, _draw_wmo_exterior, _draw_terrain, _draw_water,
   _draw_vertex_color and _draw_model_animations, read off the
   BoolToggleProperty initialisers in MapView.h.

   Same recess model as the tool strip, rotated: the rule goes on the BOTTOM
   edge at 2px and the left reserve drops to 1px. Eight recessed dark wells
   with gold glyphs along a bar reads as a row of latched switches, which is
   exactly what it is -- louder than a bare underline, quieter than eight
   saturated boxes, because the loudness is carried by a DARK plate rather than
   by a saturated one.

   BOX 34x34, content-rect arithmetic as above:
     width  = 1 + 6 + 20 + 6 + 1                      = 34
     height = 1 (top) + 5 + 23 + 3 + 2 (bottom rule)  = 34
   The bottom padding is 3px rather than 5px precisely so the 2px rule is paid
   for and the box does not change height between states. Measured 34x34.

   Scoped by CLASS NAME rather than by QToolBar:horizontal, which Qt parses and
   then throws away. The class is safe to scope on: the two other ViewToolbar
   instances are filled entirely with QWidgetActions and contain no
   QToolButton. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton {
	background: transparent;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 1px solid transparent;
	border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
	border-radius: 5px;
	padding: 5px 6px 3px 6px;
	min-width: 20px;
	max-width: 20px;
	min-height: 23px;
	max-height: 23px;
	icon-size: 20px;
}

/* OFF, pointer on it: the plate LIFTS to bg.hover, 1.292:1 over the card, with
   a stroke.hi edge at 1.84:1. Icon derives to #E6E3DD, 5.66:1 on the plate. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton:hover {
	background-color: #5B5651;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-left: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #746D64;
}

/* ON: the face RECESSES to bg.void, 2.057:1 below the card, and the bottom
   rule turns accent at 4.27:1 against the card. Icon accent, 8.77:1. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton:checked {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 1px solid transparent;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #DFA52E;
}

/* ON, pointer on it: lifted off the resting ON face to bg.alt (1.863:1 recess,
   so still clearly down) with the stroke.hi edge and the accent rule held.
   Icon accent 7.95:1. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton:checked:hover {
	background-color: #1D1916;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-left: 1px solid #746D64;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #DFA52E;
}

/* :pressed ties with :hover and :checked on specificity and wins on document
   order; :checked:pressed ties with :checked:hover and wins the same way. Both
   are spelled out. The ON rule is held at full accent so a press cannot erase
   the state it is about to change. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton:pressed {
	background-color: #B8801F;
	color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #B8801F;
	border-left: 1px solid #B8801F;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #B8801F;
}

Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton:checked:pressed {
	background-color: #B8801F;
	color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #B8801F;
	border-left: 1px solid #B8801F;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #DFA52E;
}

/* Last of the single-pseudo rules, so it beats :checked on document order and
   a disabled toggle shows no state mark. */
Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar QToolButton:disabled {
	background: transparent;
	color: #7F786A;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-left: 1px solid transparent;
	border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
}

Noggit--Ui--Tools--ViewToolbar--Ui--ViewToolbar::separator {
	background-color: #746D64;
	margin: 5px 4px;
}

QWidget#gizmoBar QPushButton {
	background: transparent;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
	border-radius: 5px;
	min-width: 28px;
	min-height: 28px;
	padding: 2px;
	font-weight: 600;
	font-size: 13px;
}

QWidget#gizmoBar QPushButton:hover {
	background-color: #5B5651;
	border: 1px solid #746D64;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

/* Same recess model as the two bars beside it, so the three read as one
   family: the checked face drops to bg.void and the glyph turns accent. */
QWidget#gizmoBar QPushButton:checked {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #DFA52E;
	color: #DFA52E;
}

QWidget#gizmoBar QPushButton:disabled {
	background: transparent;
	color: #7F786A;
	border: 1px solid transparent;
}

QPushButton#cameraXButton,
QPushButton#cameraYButton,
QPushButton#cameraZButton,
QPushButton#gizmoModeButton,
QPushButton#gizmoRotateButton,
QPushButton#gizmoScaleButton,
QPushButton#gizmoTranslateButton,
QPushButton#gizmoVisibleButton {
	font-weight: 600;
	font-size: 13px;
	border-radius: 5px;
}

/* Overlay sliders and labels are drawn straight over the render. */
QSlider[accessibleName="overlay_slider_vertical"] {
	background: transparent;
	padding: 12px 5px;
}

QSlider[accessibleName="overlay_slider_horizontal"] {
	background: transparent;
	padding: 5px 12px;
}

QLabel[accessibleName="overlay_label"] {
	background: transparent;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	padding: 0px 10px;
	font-size: 12px;
	font-weight: 600;
}

/* --- texture and asset browsers ----------------------------------------- */

/* Selection here must not fill the cell: the cell IS the texture. An accent
   outline reads as selected without hiding what was selected.

   HALF OF THIS IS DEAD, and it is the same Q_OBJECT problem as the left tool
   strip. Noggit::Ui::TextureList has the macro and is reachable;
   Noggit::Ui::PaletteList does not -- only texture_palette_small in that file
   does -- so Qt sees the class as a plain QListWidget and every PaletteList
   rule below is discarded. One Q_OBJECT line in that header makes all four
   live. Reproduce with:
     python check_qss.py theme.qss . --moc <build>/noggit_autogen
   The Noggit-Ui-- single-dash spellings are what the shipped Dark theme used;
   they match nothing in any build and are kept only so a reader comparing the
   two sheets can see the pair. */
Noggit--Ui--PaletteList::item,
Noggit-Ui--PaletteList::item,
Noggit--Ui--TextureList::item,
Noggit-Ui--TextureList::item {
	background: transparent;
	border: 2px solid transparent;
	border-radius: 5px;
	padding: 1px;
}

Noggit--Ui--PaletteList::item:hover,
Noggit-Ui--PaletteList::item:hover,
Noggit--Ui--TextureList::item:hover,
Noggit-Ui--TextureList::item:hover {
	background: transparent;
	border: 2px solid #746D64;
}

Noggit--Ui--PaletteList::item:selected,
Noggit-Ui--PaletteList::item:selected,
Noggit--Ui--TextureList::item:selected,
Noggit-Ui--TextureList::item:selected {
	background: transparent;
	border: 2px solid #DFA52E;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QTreeView[accessibleName="ab_treeview"]::item {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
	padding: 5px 4px;
}

QTreeView[accessibleName="ab_treeview"]::item:hover {
	background-color: #292621;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QTreeView[accessibleName="ab_treeview"]::item:selected {
	background-color: rgba(223, 165, 46, 0.24);
	border-left: 2px solid #DFA52E;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

/* The texture brush level slider paints a value ramp; it is the one control in
   the app whose groove is DATA rather than chrome, and therefore the one
   gradient in this file. Neither end is pure: #F2F0EC and #0C0A08, because the
   editor has to stay readable beside a bright viewport. The widget sets this
   name with setObjectName while the shipped sheets only matched accessibleName,
   which is why this control has apparently never rendered as designed. Both
   spellings are here. */
QSlider#texturing_brush_level_slider::groove:vertical,
QSlider[accessibleName="texturing_brush_level_slider"]::groove:vertical {
	background-color: qlineargradient(x1:0.5, y1:0, x2:0.5, y2:1,
	                                  stop:0 #F2F0EC, stop:1 #0C0A08);
	border: 1px solid #565049;
	border-radius: 5px;
	width: 30px;
	margin: 0px;
}

QSlider#texturing_brush_level_slider::handle:vertical,
QSlider[accessibleName="texturing_brush_level_slider"]::handle:vertical {
	background-color: #DFA52E;
	border: 1px solid #100E0B;
	border-radius: 2px;
	height: 6px;
	width: 34px;
	margin: 0px -2px;
}

QSlider#texturing_brush_level_slider::add-page:vertical,
QSlider#texturing_brush_level_slider::sub-page:vertical,
QSlider[accessibleName="texturing_brush_level_slider"]::add-page:vertical,
QSlider[accessibleName="texturing_brush_level_slider"]::sub-page:vertical {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
}

/* --- main menu / project list ------------------------------------------- */

QWidget[accessibleName="main_menu_minimap_holder"] {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #403B35;
	border-radius: 8px;
}

/* The two headings on the project selection window -- "Recent Projects" and
   "Getting Started". They head a whole window column rather than a section
   inside a panel, so they take 17/600 text.hi, one rank above the 15px window
   title. They used to carry an INLINE style sheet set from
   NoggitProjectSelectionWindow.cpp, which outranks the application sheet; that
   inline sheet is gone and this is the rule that dresses them now. */
QLabel#project-section-title {
	color: #F3F0E9;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 17px;
	font-weight: 600;
	padding: 0px 0px 6px 0px;
}

/* The recent-projects list. Every row is a ProjectListItem widget laid over
   the item, so the ROW draws the hover and the ITEM draws the selection: the
   widget background is transparent, which is what lets the view own selection
   wash show through from underneath it.

   THE WELL IS WHAT FIXED THE ROW. In the previous sheet this list sat on
   bg.base and its five selection states measured 5.38 / 4.75 / 4.35 / 3.80 /
   4.33 for the secondary caption -- three of them under the 4.5 floor -- which
   is what the extra `text.mid` token existed to paper over. On a bg.void well
   the same five states measure, for text.dim:

     row at rest            #100E0B    9.70
     row hovered            #292621    7.58
     row selected, active   #423213    6.23
     row selected + hovered #564117    4.88
     row selected, inactive #3C3732    5.92

   The worst reading in the matrix is 4.88:1 against 3.80:1 before, so the
   caption goes back to text.dim and text.mid is deleted rather than
   maintained. */
QListWidget[accessibleName="project_list"] {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	border: 1px solid #403B35;
	border-radius: 8px;
	padding: 4px;
}

QListWidget[accessibleName="project_list"]::item {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
	border-radius: 5px;
	padding: 0px;
	margin: 2px 2px;
}

QListWidget[accessibleName="project_list"]::item:selected:active {
	background-color: rgba(223, 165, 46, 0.24);
	border-left: 2px solid #DFA52E;
}

QListWidget[accessibleName="project_list"]::item:selected:!active {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	border-left: 2px solid #746D64;
}

/* The row widget itself. It is a plain QWidget, so it only paints a style
   sheet background because the constructor sets WA_StyledBackground.

   Hover is a TRANSLUCENT lift rather than an opaque plate, deliberately: an
   opaque fill here would cover the selection wash the view painted underneath,
   and hovering the selected row would make it look deselected. */
QWidget#project-list-item {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 5px;
}

QWidget#project-list-item:hover {
	background-color: rgba(228, 223, 215, 0.08);
}

QLabel#project-title-label {
	color: #F3F0E9;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 14px;
	font-weight: 600;
	padding: 0px;
}

/* Back on text.dim: see the five-state table on the list rule above. */
QLabel#project-information {
	color: #BFB7AA;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 11px;
	padding: 0px;
}

QLabel#project-icon-label {
	background: transparent;
	padding: 0px;
}

/* A favourite or a pin is a marked state, not a warning, so it takes the
   accent: 6.86:1 on the panel and 8.77:1 over the bg.void list well. */
QLabel#project-favorite,
QLabel#project-pinned {
	background: transparent;
	color: #DFA52E;
}

QPushButton#importPathField_browse,
QPushButton#wmvLogPathField_browse {
	margin-left: 8px;
}

/* --- settings window ----------------------------------------------------- */

/* "Changes may not take effect until next launch" is the only thing in the
   window that explains why a setting the user just saved appears to do
   nothing, so it stays at body rank -- 12px text, 11.36:1 on the panel --
   rather than being demoted to a caption. The hierarchy in that footer is
   settled by the Save button taking the primary treatment, which is a
   difference in KIND, not by making the sentence quieter. */
QLabel#_restart_hint {
	color: #E4DFD7;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 12px;
	margin-right: 12px;
}

QPushButton#searchButton {
	padding: 5px 12px;
	margin-right: 4px;
}

/* The collapsible section headers ExpanderWidget builds -- "Custom Brush" and
   friends. It still sets one inline declaration, text-align: left, and that
   wins over this rule for that property alone; it has to, because QPushButton
   has no setAlignment and a theme that said nothing would centre a section
   caption.

   It takes the QGroupBox heading treatment verbatim -- now 13/700 text.hi on a
   stroke rule -- because it IS the same rank. "Custom Brush" heads a section
   exactly the way "Settings" does, and the only reason the two ever looked
   different is that one is a QGroupBox and the other is a QPushButton
   pretending to be one. */
QPushButton#__qt__passive_button {
	background: transparent;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: none;
	border-top: 1px solid #565049;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
	border-radius: 0px;
	padding: 8px 8px;
	font-size: 13px;
	font-weight: 700;
	text-align: left;
}

QPushButton#__qt__passive_button:hover {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

QPushButton#__qt__passive_button:checked {
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border-left: 2px solid #DFA52E;
}

/* --- ground effects tool -------------------------------------------------- */

/* The four weight cells under a ground-effect set. The tool used to draw them
   with an inline "1px solid darkGray", the Qt colour name -- #A9A9A9, brighter
   than any text colour in this palette. The list takes NoSelection, so the
   cell is a container and not a control: stroke, not stroke.hi. */
QListWidget[accessibleName="ge_weight_list"]::item {
	border: 1px solid #565049;
	border-radius: 5px;
	padding-right: 6px;
}

/* --- key bindings window ------------------------------------------------- */

/* The seventeen section headings in Help > Key Bindings. The window sets bold
   through QFont and leaves everything else here; it used to carry an inline
   sheet whose margin-left: 150px pushed each heading into the middle of the
   rows it labels. margin-top is the rhythm between one section and the next --
   QSS margins on a QLabel are honoured by the form layout as extra row height,
   which is why the spacing does not need a spacer item. */
QLabel#help-section-header {
	color: #F3F0E9;
	background: transparent;
	font-size: 13px;
	font-weight: 700;
	margin-top: 12px;
	margin-bottom: 4px;
	margin-left: 0px;
}

/* --- map creation wizard ------------------------------------------------- */

QPushButton[accessibleName="map_wizard_add_button"],
QPushButton[accessibleName="map_wizard_remove_button"],
QPushButton[accessibleName="map_wizard_save_button"],
QPushButton[accessibleName="map_wizard_discard_button"] {
	qproperty-iconSize: 14px 14px;
	padding: 5px 12px;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="map_wizard_remove_button"] {
	margin-left: 8px;
}

QPushButton[accessibleName="map_wizard_save_button"] {
	margin-right: 8px;
}

/* --- colour widgets ------------------------------------------------------ */

color_widgets--ColorSelector {
	min-width: 100px;
	max-height: 28px;
	border: 1px solid #565049;
	border-radius: 5px;
}

color_widgets--ColorPreview {
	border: 1px solid #565049;
	border-radius: 5px;
}

color_widgets--ColorWheel {
	background: transparent;
	padding: 16px;
}

/* A QLineEdit subclass, so it needs the same padding the base class needs to
   come out 28px tall. */
color_widgets--ColorLineEdit {
	background-color: #100E0B;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border: 1px solid #565049;
	border-radius: 5px;
	padding: 1px 8px;
	min-height: 16px;
}


/* ------------------------------------------- advanced docking system (ADS) -- */

/* Linked but not currently instantiated. Kept short so that if it ever is, it
   does not appear unstyled. */

ads--CDockContainerWidget,
ads--CDockAreaWidget,
ads--CDockWidget {
	background-color: #292621;
	border: none;
}

ads--CDockContainerWidget ads--CDockSplitter::handle {
	background-color: #100E0B;
}

ads--CDockAreaTitleBar {
	background-color: #292621;
	border: none;
	border-bottom: 1px solid #403B35;
}

ads--CDockWidgetTab {
	background: transparent;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	border: none;
	border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
	padding: 6px 12px;
	max-height: 30px;
	font-size: 13px;
	font-weight: 700;
}

ads--CDockWidgetTab:hover {
	background-color: #3C3732;
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

ads--CDockWidgetTab[activeTab="true"] {
	background: transparent;
	color: #F3F0E9;
	border-bottom: 2px solid #DFA52E;
}

ads--CDockWidgetTab QLabel {
	color: #BFB7AA;
	background: transparent;
}

ads--CDockWidgetTab[activeTab="true"] QLabel {
	color: #F3F0E9;
}

ads--CTitleBarButton {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-radius: 5px;
	padding: 2px;
}

ads--CTitleBarButton:hover {
	background-color: #3C3732;
}

QToolButton#tabCloseButton,
QToolButton#dockAreaCloseButton {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
	qproperty-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_close.png);
	qproperty-iconSize: 10px 10px;
}

QToolButton#tabsMenuButton,
QToolButton#detachGroupButton {
	background: transparent;
	border: none;
	border-left: 2px solid transparent;
	qproperty-icon: url(themes/CrimsonSlate/images/icon_undock.png);
	qproperty-iconSize: 14px 14px;
}

QToolButton#tabsMenuButton::menu-indicator {
	image: none;
	width: 0px;
	height: 0px;
}

QLabel#floatingTitleLabel,
QLabel#dockWidgetTabLabel,
QLabel#DockWidgetAreaLabel {
	background: transparent;
	color: #BFB7AA;
	font-size: 11px;
	font-weight: 700;
}
